WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.333 --> 00:00:01.668
Chief business correspondent Christine

00:00:01.668 --> 00:00:03.036
Romans is here with us.

00:00:03.036 --> 00:00:03.937
Christine,

00:00:03.937 --> 00:00:05.105
if you weren't paying attention

00:00:05.105 --> 00:00:05.472
on Friday

00:00:05.472 --> 00:00:06.973
afternoon, you were off work

00:00:06.973 --> 00:00:08.174
or on a trip,

00:00:08.174 --> 00:00:08.842
break down

00:00:08.842 --> 00:00:10.377
exactly what happened

00:00:10.377 --> 00:00:12.045
here would be the anatomy

00:00:12.045 --> 00:00:14.247
of a collapse of a big bank

00:00:14.414 --> 00:00:15.982
and the anatomy of a rescue

00:00:15.982 --> 00:00:16.850
from the federal government.

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:18.284
Let's look at how we got here.

00:00:18.284 --> 00:00:21.021
First of all, what is as VB?

00:00:21.021 --> 00:00:23.690
It is the epicenter of tech startups,

00:00:23.723 --> 00:00:25.725
venture capital, Silicon Valley Bank.

00:00:25.892 --> 00:00:27.494
It is the 16th largest bank

00:00:27.494 --> 00:00:29.496
or it was in the United States

00:00:29.496 --> 00:00:31.097
and this is really

00:00:31.097 --> 00:00:34.000
the oxygen for technology.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.368
The technology sector

00:00:35.368 --> 00:00:37.437
really goes through SVP.

00:00:37.537 --> 00:00:39.039
So a very important

00:00:39.039 --> 00:00:41.941
bank here by the end of 20, 22.

00:00:42.075 --> 00:00:44.744
It had $175 billion in total deposits.

00:00:44.911 --> 00:00:47.280
And here is the anatomy of the bank run.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:48.948
Right. This is how much money it had.

00:00:48.948 --> 00:00:49.716
End of the year.

00:00:49.716 --> 00:00:51.184
Just on Thursday

00:00:51.184 --> 00:00:53.219
there were $42 billion

00:00:53.386 --> 00:00:55.588
in withdrawals from depositors. Right.

00:00:55.622 --> 00:00:56.723
So something happened here

00:00:56.723 --> 00:00:58.358
where people want to take their money out

00:00:58.358 --> 00:01:00.560
and this bank unraveled here.

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:03.063
So what happened? Why did they get there?

00:01:03.263 --> 00:01:04.297
Well, this is a company

00:01:04.297 --> 00:01:05.765
that for years took deposits

00:01:05.765 --> 00:01:06.666
in the good times

00:01:06.666 --> 00:01:08.301
when interest rates were very low

00:01:08.301 --> 00:01:10.003
and it grew and grew and grew

00:01:10.003 --> 00:01:11.371
and those deposits grew.

00:01:11.371 --> 00:01:12.472
And it put that money

00:01:12.472 --> 00:01:14.340
into long dated treasuries,

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:15.375
into treasuries, safe,

00:01:15.375 --> 00:01:17.577
super safe treasuries. Right.

00:01:17.744 --> 00:01:19.145
So this is how much they had

00:01:19.145 --> 00:01:20.080
at the end of 20,

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:24.084
22, $128 billion worth of of Treasury.

00:01:24.084 --> 00:01:24.317
Look at

00:01:24.317 --> 00:01:26.619
that is amazing how much it gained.

00:01:26.619 --> 00:01:28.588
But then as things started to go south,

00:01:28.588 --> 00:01:29.656
when interest rates were rising,

00:01:29.656 --> 00:01:31.191
those were less valuable

00:01:31.191 --> 00:01:32.692
and the company actually

00:01:32.692 --> 00:01:34.861
was selling those treasuries at a loss.

00:01:34.861 --> 00:01:36.529
When that word got out.

00:01:36.529 --> 00:01:37.464
People got nervous.

00:01:37.464 --> 00:01:39.399
The stock in the bank started to decline.

00:01:39.399 --> 00:01:41.501
Depositors started to withdraw their

00:01:41.501 --> 00:01:41.968
their money.

00:01:41.968 --> 00:01:42.969
They were also withdrawing their money

00:01:42.969 --> 00:01:43.837
because technology

00:01:43.837 --> 00:01:46.039
that sector was in a downturn. Right.

00:01:46.139 --> 00:01:47.040
So instead of putting money

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:47.974
into the bank,

00:01:47.974 --> 00:01:49.042
depositors are taking money

00:01:49.042 --> 00:01:50.310
out of the bank and the bank

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:51.744
could not sustain it.

00:01:51.744 --> 00:01:52.779
Come to the rescue.

00:01:52.779 --> 00:01:53.746
The United States government,

00:01:53.746 --> 00:01:56.049
the FDIC, is going to insure up

00:01:56.049 --> 00:01:58.218
to $250,000 in normal times.

00:01:58.318 --> 00:01:58.885
And now you're

00:01:58.885 --> 00:01:59.853
going to have this facility

00:01:59.853 --> 00:02:01.354
so that above that level,

00:02:01.354 --> 00:02:02.555
everyone will be made whole,

00:02:02.555 --> 00:02:03.723
not the bondholders

00:02:03.723 --> 00:02:05.125
and not the stockholders of the bank,

00:02:05.125 --> 00:02:06.259
we should point out,

00:02:06.259 --> 00:02:08.228
but the people who have their deposits

00:02:08.228 --> 00:02:10.363
in that bank, also signature bank.

00:02:10.363 --> 00:02:11.798
This is the other

00:02:11.798 --> 00:02:13.766
the other shoe to drop this weekend.

00:02:13.766 --> 00:02:14.434
This is a bank

00:02:14.434 --> 00:02:16.169
that specializes in services

00:02:16.169 --> 00:02:17.103
for law firms

00:02:17.103 --> 00:02:19.339
It also got into big trouble.

00:02:19.472 --> 00:02:20.874
The government will also back

00:02:20.874 --> 00:02:21.875
the depositors there.

00:02:21.875 --> 00:02:23.209
So a really important

00:02:23.209 --> 00:02:24.544
weekend of developments, you guys.

00:02:24.544 --> 00:02:25.111
This could have been

00:02:25.111 --> 00:02:26.613
a very different morning.

00:02:26.613 --> 00:02:27.547
A very different morning

00:02:27.547 --> 00:02:28.781
if we didn't have the Treasury

00:02:28.781 --> 00:02:29.983
and the Fed working all weekend

00:02:29.983 --> 00:02:31.117
to try to make sure

00:02:31.117 --> 00:02:32.519
that folks were made whole.

00:02:32.519 --> 00:02:34.387
And and I'll say this is because

00:02:34.387 --> 00:02:35.221
interest rates rose

00:02:35.221 --> 00:02:36.623
so dramatically as something

00:02:36.623 --> 00:02:38.791
was going to break in the economy. Right.

00:02:38.791 --> 00:02:40.760
And this is what broke, guys.

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:42.061
And that comes as the Fed

00:02:42.061 --> 00:02:42.962
chair is only warning

00:02:42.962 --> 00:02:44.497
that federal interest rates

00:02:44.497 --> 00:02:45.498
are going to continue

00:02:45.498 --> 00:02:45.999
to rise

00:02:45.999 --> 00:02:46.533
Christine Romans

00:02:46.533 --> 00:02:47.500
will wait to see what the markets

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:49.435
say when they open. Thank you.

00:02:49.435 --> 00:02:51.538
With us now, CNN business correspondent

00:02:51.538 --> 00:02:52.172
Michelle Solomon

00:02:52.172 --> 00:02:53.806
and Wall Street Journal senior

00:02:53.806 --> 00:02:56.075
special writer Justin Baer.

00:02:56.075 --> 00:02:57.277
It just by the way, Justin's

00:02:57.277 --> 00:02:58.511
latest reporting is headlined

00:02:58.511 --> 00:02:59.913
How Silicon Valley Turned on

00:02:59.913 --> 00:03:01.581
Silicon Valley Bank.

00:03:01.581 --> 00:03:03.216
So good to have both of you on.

00:03:03.216 --> 00:03:05.552
You know, I was reading your notes

00:03:05.552 --> 00:03:06.753
and what you wrote about this.

00:03:06.753 --> 00:03:09.155
You have been very strong about this.

00:03:09.155 --> 00:03:10.623
And at the end, tough

00:03:10.623 --> 00:03:11.624
on what you call

00:03:11.624 --> 00:03:13.593
you are calling this a bailout.

00:03:13.593 --> 00:03:14.160
You said there's

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:14.961
no other way of putting it.

00:03:14.961 --> 00:03:15.461
This is a bailout.

00:03:15.461 --> 00:03:16.429
No matter what terminology

00:03:16.429 --> 00:03:17.430
