00:08:00.446 --> 00:08:01.948
I think the big difference here is that

00:08:01.948 --> 00:08:03.182
this was largely

00:08:03.182 --> 00:08:05.384
a very sort of insulated bank. Right.

00:08:05.384 --> 00:08:06.552
A big bank, nonetheless,

00:08:06.552 --> 00:08:08.788
but sort of focusing on a specific sector

00:08:08.788 --> 00:08:10.490
of the economy, a very mature

00:08:10.490 --> 00:08:11.324
public is a

00:08:11.324 --> 00:08:13.259
very important part of the economy,

00:08:13.259 --> 00:08:15.228
but a secular,

00:08:15.228 --> 00:08:16.696
sort of insulated part of the economy.

00:08:16.696 --> 00:08:18.764
I think the reason why the Fed stepped up

00:08:18.764 --> 00:08:19.832
as quickly as they did

00:08:19.832 --> 00:08:21.467
is to prevent the risk to people

00:08:21.467 --> 00:08:23.669
like everyone watching at home. Right.

00:08:23.703 --> 00:08:25.738
To protect other regional banks

00:08:25.905 --> 00:08:27.206
so that we have confidence

00:08:27.206 --> 00:08:28.174
in the banking system

00:08:28.174 --> 00:08:29.442
and that's why we saw the type of

00:08:29.442 --> 00:08:30.910
rapid response that we did.

00:08:30.910 --> 00:08:33.079
I don't know just and I mean,

00:08:33.079 --> 00:08:33.579
you point

00:08:33.579 --> 00:08:35.982
first year public was down like 30%

00:08:36.215 --> 00:08:37.116
that we have images

00:08:37.116 --> 00:08:38.818
of these long lines of people

00:08:38.818 --> 00:08:40.520
going to first republic over the weekend

00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:42.488
trying to take money out there

00:08:42.488 --> 00:08:45.525
they're standing that signature's not

00:08:45.791 --> 00:08:47.360
and these are not just banks

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:49.562
that cater to Silicon Valley.

00:08:49.795 --> 00:08:50.763
You wrote in your piece

00:08:50.763 --> 00:08:52.899
about how this exposes

00:08:52.899 --> 00:08:54.367
some of the vulnerabilities

00:08:54.367 --> 00:08:56.269
that we still have.

00:08:56.269 --> 00:08:57.036
Sure.

00:08:57.303 --> 00:08:58.070
As you said earlier

00:08:58.070 --> 00:08:59.639
I mean, this all based on confidence.

00:08:59.639 --> 00:08:59.906
Right.

00:08:59.906 --> 00:09:01.073
And the calm

00:09:01.073 --> 00:09:02.608
in the whole financial system.

00:09:02.608 --> 00:09:04.343
Any financial institution

00:09:04.343 --> 00:09:05.344
is dependent on that,

00:09:05.344 --> 00:09:06.679
on the ongoing confidence.

00:09:06.679 --> 00:09:08.414
So once that's lost or once

00:09:08.414 --> 00:09:09.882
it becomes lost,

00:09:09.882 --> 00:09:11.884
it can lead to what we saw this

00:09:11.951 --> 00:09:13.419
this past week. Right.

00:09:13.419 --> 00:09:14.053
And that can

00:09:14.053 --> 00:09:16.155
that can flare up elsewhere very quickly,

00:09:16.522 --> 00:09:16.989
which again, is

00:09:16.989 --> 00:09:17.990
why you saw the government

00:09:17.990 --> 00:09:18.658
go beyond

00:09:18.658 --> 00:09:20.760
just looking at these two institutions

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:21.594
over the weekend,

00:09:21.594 --> 00:09:24.063
but saying, well, how do we avoid

00:09:24.063 --> 00:09:25.197
seeing other lenders,

00:09:25.197 --> 00:09:26.299
particularly those that

00:09:26.299 --> 00:09:27.066
that, again,

00:09:27.066 --> 00:09:28.434
the regulations have changed

00:09:28.434 --> 00:09:29.669
over the past decade

00:09:29.669 --> 00:09:30.870
with respect to smaller

00:09:30.870 --> 00:09:32.438
and medium sized banks.

00:09:32.438 --> 00:09:34.740
How can we protect and insulate

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:35.841
those not only those banks,

00:09:35.841 --> 00:09:37.043
but more importantly,

00:09:37.043 --> 00:09:38.411
their customers are smaller,

00:09:38.411 --> 00:09:39.345
medium sized banks?

00:09:39.345 --> 00:09:41.013
Or is it because they're holders

00:09:41.013 --> 00:09:43.282
of a lot of mortgages, for example?

00:09:43.950 --> 00:09:45.017
I mean, all the above, right?

00:09:45.017 --> 00:09:46.652
You know, so so

00:09:46.652 --> 00:09:48.187
you know, every about every bank

00:09:48.187 --> 00:09:48.688
balance sheet

00:09:48.688 --> 00:09:49.255
is going to look

00:09:49.255 --> 00:09:50.489
a little bit differently.

00:09:50.489 --> 00:09:50.790
Right.

00:09:50.790 --> 00:09:51.290
I mean,

00:09:51.290 --> 00:09:52.525
everyone has had to deal

00:09:52.525 --> 00:09:55.361
with the impact of rising interest rates.

00:09:55.695 --> 00:09:56.262
Some did.

00:09:56.262 --> 00:09:57.129
So obviously

00:09:57.129 --> 00:09:58.698
a lot more prudently than than others.

00:09:58.698 --> 00:09:59.532
Right. Right.

00:09:59.532 --> 00:10:00.733
And so there will be others

00:10:00.733 --> 00:10:03.002
that will resemble in some ways

00:10:03.002 --> 00:10:04.937
the way Silicon Valley Bank looked.

00:10:04.937 --> 00:10:05.338
Right.

00:10:05.338 --> 00:10:07.440
You and we see some of the other names

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:08.207
get very quickly

00:10:08.207 --> 00:10:09.475
attached to what happened

00:10:09.475 --> 00:10:10.443
to them last week.

00:10:11.377 --> 00:10:12.912
But but again, we'll have to see.

00:10:12.912 --> 00:10:14.647
It's all based on confidence.

00:10:14.647 --> 00:10:15.381
It's going to be interesting

00:10:15.381 --> 00:10:16.048
to see what happens

00:10:16.048 --> 00:10:17.450
as we get closer to the markets

00:10:17.450 --> 00:10:19.518
and the day the banks start opening up.

00:10:19.652 --> 00:10:20.252
And some of them

00:10:20.252 --> 00:10:21.387
have actually opened up higher.

00:10:21.387 --> 00:10:21.954
I mean, some of them

00:10:21.954 --> 00:10:24.023
have actually been higher on this event.

00:10:24.023 --> 00:10:24.824
We're going to be watching that.

00:10:24.824 --> 00:10:25.758
The president's going to speak.

00:10:25.758 --> 00:10:27.026
There's going to be a lot to deal with

00:10:27.026 --> 00:10:27.326
with this.

00:10:27.326 --> 00:10:28.527
Thank you, Michelle. Thank you.

00:10:28.527 --> 00:10:29.629
Justin, we appreciate that.

